<main class="layout-container layout-stack-main">

  <header class="surface-bento page-hero-bento">
    <h1 class="text-page-title">Decoding the King County Sewer Capacity Charge</h1>
    <p class="text-body page-hero-lead">
      If you have recently purchased a newly constructed home, an upgraded property, or a home built after 1990 in the Puget Sound region, you might receive an independent bill from King County for a "Sewer Capacity Charge." Here is what this fee means, how long it lasts, and how to navigate your payment options.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="section-divider"></div>

  <section class="content-section-centered">
    <h2 class="text-section-title">The "Why": What Are You Actually Paying For?</h2>
    <p class="text-body section-body-prose">
      It is common for buyers to look at their first capacity statement and wonder why they are receiving a separate bill when they already pay a monthly water and sewer utility bill.
    </p>
    <p class="text-body section-body-prose">
      Your local municipal or water district utility bill maintains the localized network of neighborhood pipes beneath your street that collects wastewater and moves it away from your property. The King County Capacity Charge funds the regional infrastructure required to transport and treat millions of gallons of wastewater before returning it to Puget Sound. Because new construction and property expansions increase regional load, municipal law requires that growth pays for growth, shielding existing residents from financing major treatment plant expansions such as the Brightwater facility in Woodinville.
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="section-divider"></div>

  <section class="content-section-centered">
    <h2 class="text-section-title">The 15-Year Clock &amp; Billing Cadence</h2>
    <div class="section-grid-static grid-cols-2">
      
      <article class="surface-bento pathway-item-card">
        <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Duration</div>
        <h3 class="text-widget-title">The 15-Year Fixed Clock</h3>
        <p class="text-body-secondary pathway-item-desc">
          The capacity charge is not a permanent property tax. It is a fixed, 15-year capitalization fee (180 months total). Once a property completes its final side sewer hookup inspection, the account opens and runs for exactly 15 years. Once completed, the charge drops off the parcel permanently.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="surface-bento pathway-item-card">
        <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Invoicing</div>
        <h3 class="text-widget-title">Quarterly Billing Invoices</h3>
        <p class="text-body-secondary pathway-item-desc">
          King County quotes capacity fees as a monthly rate ($77.99/mo for the baseline tier), but statements are issued quarterly (four times per year). Each invoice covers three full months of service, so a $77.99/mo assessment results in a recurring $233.97 quarterly bill.
        </p>
      </article>

    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="section-divider"></div>

  <section class="content-section-centered">
    <h2 class="text-section-title">The Cross-County Mystery: Snohomish County Properties</h2>
    <div class="surface-bento">
      <p class="text-body section-body-prose">
        Many properties physically situated inside Snohomish County (such as portions of Bothell, Mill Creek, Lynnwood, and Mountlake Terrace) are billed directly by King County.
      </p>
      <p class="text-body section-body-prose">
        Wastewater systems follow natural gravity slopes within regional watersheds rather than administrative county lines. When a Snohomish County neighborhood connects through a local provider like Alderwood Water and Wastewater District that routes effluent into King County trunk lines or the Brightwater regional treatment facility, King County assesses the capacity charge based on treatment infrastructure utilization.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="section-divider"></div>

  <section class="content-section-centered">
    <h2 class="text-section-title">The 2026 Tiered Allocation Matrix</h2>
    <p class="text-body section-body-prose">
      For the 2026 calendar year, the baseline rate for a standard home is set at $77.99 per month. King County calculates individual statements by evaluating structural configuration against a standardized Residential Customer Equivalent (RCE) scale.
    </p>

    <div class="calc-table-wrap">
      <table class="timeline-data-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Property Configuration Type</th>
            <th scope="col">RCE Factor</th>
