  <section class="content-section-centered">
    <h2 class="text-section-title">Should You Pay Off the Balance Early?</h2>
    <div class="section-grid-static grid-cols-2">
      
      <article class="surface-bento pathway-item-card">
        <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Payment Mechanics</div>
        <h3 class="text-widget-title">The Extra Payment Trap</h3>
        <p class="text-body-secondary pathway-item-desc">
          The capacity charge does not function like an amortized mortgage. Sending extra funds along with a standard quarterly payment does not reduce principal or trigger early payoff discounts. King County simply applies extra funds as an advance billing credit. Obtaining an early discount requires a formal, single lump-sum payoff request.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="surface-bento pathway-item-card">
        <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Lien Ownership</div>
        <h3 class="text-widget-title">The Length-of-Stay Factor</h3>
        <p class="text-body-secondary pathway-item-desc">
          The sewer capacity charge is an official property lien attached directly to the parcel, not to you as an individual. Homeowners planning to remain in the property for the full 15-year window save on long-term carrying costs by paying off the balance early. However, homeowners anticipating a move within 3 to 5 years can pass the remaining quarterly payment obligation directly to the next buyer upon sale.
        </p>
      </article>

    </div>
  </section>

